# 🐷 Sea Pig (`seapig`)

> **Detect flaky unit tests locally before they hit your CI pipeline.**

Flaky tests produce non-deterministic results for the exact same code and input. They waste developer time, clog up pull requests, burn CI budgets, and erode trust in your test suite. **Sea Pig** isolates and flags non-deterministic tests directly on your local machine before you commit.

---

## ⭐ Why Sea Pig?

| Traditional Flake Detectors | 🐷 Sea Pig |
| :--- | :--- |
| Closed source / Proprietary | **100% Open-Source (Apache 2.0)** |
| Expensive SaaS subscriptions | **Local-First & Free** |
| Tied strictly to CI/CD pipelines | **Runs instantly in your local terminal** |
| Single-language focus | **Language Agnostic** |

---

## 🧪 How It Works

Sea Pig executes your unit test suite concurrently across multiple loop iterations under identical inputs. If a test passes on one run and fails on another, Sea Pig immediately flags it as **flaky**, pinpoints the exact test case, and logs the result to a local embeddable database (`bbolt`).

---

## 💻 Language Support

### 🔧 Supported in v1.0
* 🟦 **Go** (`go`)
* 🐍 **Python** (`pytest`)
* ☕ **Java and Kotlin** (Maven / Gradle)
* 🟡 **JavaScript and TypeScript** (Jest)

### 🚀 Planned Future Languages
Ruby • PHP • Elixir • C# / .NET • Swift • Dart / Flutter • Rust • C / C++

Java/Kotlin builds require a JDK. Sea Pig builds its Java daemon automatically with `javac` when Maven or Gradle is unavailable. To package it manually, run one of these from the Sea Pig checkout root:

```bash
gradle -p servers jar
# or
mvn -f servers/pom.xml package
```

For Java/Kotlin test projects, run from the target project root and provide either its `gradlew`/`mvnw` wrapper or the matching build tool on `PATH`.

Please ensure if running maven, install it into your system path or it will not work. You can download maven from [here](https://maven.apache.org/download.cgi) and follow the installation instructions for your operating system.

Install JS/TS daemon dependencies from the Sea Pig checkout root:

```bash
npm ci --prefix servers
```

The daemons send test selectors over local sockets; source files remain in the target project. Windows uses loopback TCP for Python and JavaScript/TypeScript daemons. Java and Kotlin projects need a Maven or Gradle build, Python projects need pytest in the project environment or on `PATH`, and JavaScript/TypeScript projects need Jest configured.

---

## 🚀 Quickstart & Commands

```bash
# 1. Check supported languages and keywords
seapig list

# 2. Run a single sanity test execution
seapig run --lang java

# 3. Execute Sea Pig flake detection (default: 25 loops)
seapig pig --lang java --loop 5

# 4. View stored execution history and logs
seapig logs
Note: It is best to put the logs generated by seapig in a .gitignore file to avoid committing it to your repository. A later update will allow you to hide error messages to protect sensitive information from being logged.
```

⚙️ Configuration (seapig.yaml)
You can customize execution defaults by placing a seapig.yaml file in the root directory alongside your seapig executable:

YAML
# Number of concurrent test execution workers / default loop runs
workers: 10

# Timeout per individual test execution (e.g. 5s, 30s, 1m)
timeout: 1m
Note: Running seapig from the root directory of your project delivers the best auto-discovery results.

⚠️ Known Issues & Troubleshooting
Windows OneDrive File Sync
Issue: seapig may occasionally fail or experience file access errors when run inside folders actively synced by Windows OneDrive.

Workaround:

Move your local repository outside of the OneDrive sync path (e.g., C:\dev\your-project).

Alternatively, temporarily pause OneDrive syncing while running test suites.


